  "body": "### What problem are you trying to solve?\n\nWhen the browser cannot find a `<link rel=icon>` tag, it falls back to requesting `/favicon.ico`. This also affects non-HTML resources like images or PDFs.\n\nI would like to have the option to use an HTTP header to set the location of the favicon instead.\n\nUse cases:\n\n* Display the same favicon when viewing e.g. PDFs as well as HTML pages\n* Select a favicon based on the mime type of the response (e.g. `pdf.ico` for `/document.pdf`, `img.ico` for `/picture.jpg`)\n* Allow each user of a multi-user webhost (`http://example.org/~user`) to set their own favicon\n* Prevent /favicon.ico requests on pages that don't have any favicons at all (currently possible on HTML pages with `<link rel=icon href=\"data:,\">`)\n\n### What solutions exist today?\n\nNone.\n\n### How would you solve it?\n\nHonor `Link: rel=icon` headers to work the same as a `<link rel=icon>` tag would have.\n\n### Anything else?\n\n_No response_",
